  "name": null,
  "problem": "If $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-6x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x={x}_{1}=-4$ and $x={x}_{2}=2.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-4\\cdot 2=-8.\n$$",
